  "body": "The proposal expected k_recovery to move when a second object is added after the fence. The execution shows it did not: before and after both returned {\"a\":1}. The rule says the object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text. The source implements this as: for pool in ((fenced if len(fenced) == 1 else []), top_level). The fenced pool is used only when exactly one fence exists, and here there is exactly one fence, so the top_level pool is never consulted. The added object {\"b\": 2} is outside the fence, so it is not a candidate in the fenced pool, and the top_level pool is skipped. The proposer misread the rule: the rule says the last object inside a code fence when any fence holds one, and the added object is not inside a fence. The rule as written and the code agree; the failure is a misreading of the rule, not a divergence. A proposer should build a case where the added object is inside the same fence, or where the fence count changes, to test the boundary between fenced and top-level recovery.",
